WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:21.811
All of us have way too much complexity
in our lives, too many devices,

00:00:21.811 --> 00:00:23.650
our kids going a million places.

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:26.850
At work we have all kinds of
things we're supposed to be doing.

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:29.170
And realizing,
is there a way to be simple?

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:33.960
And noticing that some of, I think, the
most effective people actually are simple.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.910
And so
that kind of led us to start exploring

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:38.950
what's the value of simple rules and
simplicity?

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.100
We started studying companies and

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:46.240
their product development processes,
and we realized that

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:51.760
there were companies that had these very
complicated product development processes.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.780
And what would happen was they would do
the wrong product really efficiently.

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.620
And conversely we would see these other
companies where there were no rules at

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:03.600
all, and they were having a great
time getting nothing done.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.120
And we saw that then again,
there was this intermediate.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.780
A few simple rules, who's in charge of
what, what kind of a product do we do.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:15.431
But just maybe four or five rules
that constrained what people did but

00:01:15.431 --> 00:01:18.247
gave them some flexibility to innovate.

00:01:18.247 --> 00:01:22.775
[MUSIC]

00:01:22.775 --> 00:01:25.410
There's really three steps to
coming up with simple rules.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:27.710
One of them is what's the objective.

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:29.680
What are we really trying to achieve here?

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.420
Is it revenue, is it growth?

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:35.720
Is it notoriety?

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:40.070
So what's the underlying business
objective we're trying to achieve?

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:43.360
And so profitability and growth, for
example, would be two different things.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:44.400
So what are we trying to achieve and

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.160
that's usually fairly straightforward
with business people to come up with.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:52.060
What's harder to figure out is what's the
bottleneck process, what's the repeated

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:55.499
thing that we do often that really
keeps us from achieving that objective?

00:01:56.630 --> 00:02:00.010
So an interesting example is
actually Google, back in the day.

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:03.930
There were some problems at Google early
on, where they weren't getting enough

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:08.160
product improvement in their search
engine, and some of their other products.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:11.431
So their product development wasn't
as good as they wanted it to be.

00:02:11.431 --> 00:02:14.940
So they thought a bit about,
do we reorganize product development?

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:16.430
What do we do?

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:19.860
Well then as they thought about it more,
the real bottleneck that was keeping them

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:24.160
from being successful was the quality
of their computer scientists.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.960
And so they realized the real
bottleneck was the hiring process.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.265
And what we need to do is get
top-flight computer scientists because

00:02:30.265 --> 00:02:33.480
top-flight computer
scientists are substantially

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.530
better than the average
computer scientist.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:41.030
And then they developed some simple rules
that were things you might not expect.

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:45.170
Things like look for people who are
eccentric because they're more creative.

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:50.210
So, people who ride a unicycle,
climb the Himalayas, do something strange.

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:54.410
And look for people, always prefer
referrals from other Googlers.

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:57.470
Because the idea there was Googlers
know what good Googlers look like,

00:02:57.470 --> 00:02:59.330
and they want to work with them.

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:04.300
And then the third one was stop the hiring
process if you see anything phony or

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:08.270
fake on the resume because we
want high integrity people.

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:11.410
So the point here is that
the objective was not

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:12.950
what they first thought
it was going to be.

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:15.348
It's actually the bottleneck was not what
they first thought it was going to be,

00:03:15.348 --> 00:03:18.860
it ultimately became something
around computer science hires.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:21.390
And then finally developing the rules.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:23.890
That's the third step
is what are the rules?

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:28.760
And that's usually a combination
of looking back at your own data.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:31.932
So for example, I worked with a company
in biotech where partnerships

00:03:31.932 --> 00:03:35.588
were very important, and they looked back
at their data trying to figure out when

00:03:35.588 --> 00:03:37.930
were we successful when weren't we.

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:39.560
So trying to understand
from your own data.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:43.550
Another strategy is
bringing outside experts.

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:47.054
You come up with rules by thinking about
what your objective is, trying to figure

00:03:47.054 --> 00:03:50.368
out what that the bottleneck is,
which is probably the hardest thing to do.

00:03:50.368 --> 00:03:53.082
And then developing rules
partly your experience,

00:03:53.082 --> 00:03:55.802
partly working with outsiders
to come up with them.

00:03:55.802 --> 00:04:00.352
[MUSIC]

00:04:00.352 --> 00:04:04.385
You can make faster decisions when you're
simple because you only have to think of

00:04:04.385 --> 00:04:05.720
a few factors.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:08.990
The second reason is that there
are certain situations that

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:11.840
simplicity actually is not only faster but

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:15.870
it's actually better than using
complicated formulas and lots of data.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:20.190
Because if you use too much,
if you use a lot of data on a formula,

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:24.390
what you tend to do is overfit the past,
which is a poor predictor future.

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:30.970
So it's actually, because what you're
trying to do is predict the future.

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:33.370
And given let's say you
have a set of data and

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:36.470
there are multiple ways
you can fit that data.

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:39.990
The simplest fitting of that data
is the most likely to be predictive

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:41.070
of the future.

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:45.150
And then it's better because
people actually do it.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:51.520
So, for example, if I give you three
rules to remember for partnerships.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:55.000
Or three rules remember for
investing or dieting.

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:59.820
You are more likely to remember and
do it, even if you're stressed out,

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:04.095
you're busy, you're got a zillion things
to do, you can say, wait a minute.

00:05:04.095 --> 00:05:06.995
I'm supposed to think about hiring
people and I remember these three rules.

00:05:06.995 --> 00:05:08.425
And that's all I have to remember.

00:05:08.425 --> 00:05:11.743
So it's particularly effective when you're
just stressed out with too much to do.

00:05:11.743 --> 00:05:16.259
[MUSIC]

00:05:16.259 --> 00:05:19.960
It works best when you involve
the people in your organization.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:22.941
So not just you at the top
deciding what the rules are, but

00:05:22.941 --> 00:05:27.107
getting the talent below you thinking
about it and participating in the process

00:05:27.107 --> 00:05:30.738
of coming up with the rules,
looking at the data, testing them out.

00:05:30.738 --> 00:05:35.220
[MUSIC]

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:39.360
The stopping rules
are the hardest to learn.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:41.810
When do I back off?

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:46.720
We're trying to sell to a client and
when is it time to say no?

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:49.420
We're developing a product,
when is it time to stop?

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:51.790
We have an investment, when do we sell it?

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:55.470
Those stopping rules are the hardest for
people to learn.

00:05:55.470 --> 00:06:02.620
And in I think maybe ironically, they're
also related to higher performance.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:05.170
So, probably one

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:08.390
of the biggest mistake business people
make is they stay in something too long.

00:06:08.390 --> 00:06:11.030
And a stopping rule helps
you get out of that.

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:15.040
People are really good at starting and
are really bad at stopping.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:20.500
And so a rule that says I'm done with this
partner, I'm done with the sales call.

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:24.760
I'm done with this product, I'm done
with this person, I'm not hiring them.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:27.468
Whatever the process is that
you have rule that says I stop.

00:06:27.468 --> 00:06:32.065
[MUSIC]

00:06:32.065 --> 00:06:36.160
When it's time to change is when they
don't seem to be working anymore, or

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:39.150
the situation has somehow changed.

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:43.070
One of the examples,
I think is pretty obvious and

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:44.850
people would know well is the Moneyball.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:48.130
The Oakland A's Moneyball,
which was focused on,

00:06:48.130 --> 00:06:51.909
among other things, getting players
with high on-base percentage.

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:56.150
Well once everybody else in
the major leagues figures that out,

00:06:56.150 --> 00:06:57.370
they're doing it too.

00:06:57.370 --> 00:07:00.610
They're paying more than you are, and the
Boston Red Sox are winning the pennant,

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:01.180
and you're not.

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:07.150
And so, when your rules are starting
to be copied, by competitors.

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:10.114
When, or when the situations change,
as it did for the A's,

00:07:10.114 --> 00:07:11.660
you have to change up the rules.

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:16.499
[MUSIC]

